C. Yes, these positions can both arise in the same game. The positions can arise in any order.

The positions are identical in material, pawn skeleton, king/rook locations, castling rights, and en-passant field. The only distinctions are the four minor-piece placements produced by the reversible moves Bc1-g5/Bf1-g2 (White) and ...Nb8-d7/...Bf8-e7 (Black).  

All four routes are unobstructed in both directions:  
- c1-g5 travels the open diagonal d2-e3-f4 (d2 empty by FEN; e3/f4 unoccupied);  
- f1-g2 is a single-step diagonal through the already-advanced g-pawn;  
- b8-d7 is a direct knight leap that remains available in reverse;  
- f8-e7 is a single-step diagonal.  

No piece, pawn, or capture blocks any retreat square, and the preserved castling rights confirm the kings and rooks never moved. Consequently the same sequence of ordinary developing (or retreating) moves can be played from the initial position to reach either diagram first and then reach the other.

